# Backwards compatibility policy

Default: **do not** add backwards-compatibility code. No shims, legacy aliases,
re-exports for old names, fallback paths, deprecated wrappers, unreleased-schema
migrations, flags that only preserve unshipped behaviour, or dead code "just in
case". Change the thing and update every caller.

Compatibility is valid ONLY for a contract that reached `main` or shipped (a
released build, a published package version a consumer pins, or persisted data an
installed build already wrote). When you add a compatibility path, name what it
protects (release/tag, deployed client, persisted-data migration, package
version) and give it a removal plan.

Sovran specifics:
- **Persisted shape changes** (Zustand `persist`, SQLite) on data an installed
  build wrote ARE a shipped contract → bump `version` + add `migrate`
  (`__rules__/caching.md`), never a shim. A persisted change WITHOUT version+migrate
  is the inverse bug (a silent break).
- **colada / nagg-ts / nagg are Sovran-only** — change one, update Sovran, republish;
  no dual API / deprecation window.
- Branch-only / draft / prototype / local-test / agent-intermediate code has zero
  users — refactor freely; never "preserve" it.

Full playbook + how to determine what shipped: `skill:no-backwards-compatibility`.
